  "full_response": "Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm based on the concept of \"objects,\" which can contain data in the form of fields (often known as attributes or properties) and code in the form of procedures (often known as methods). The key principles of OOP include:\n\n1. **Encapsulation**:\n   - This principle is about bundling the data (attributes) and methods that operate on the data into a single unit called a class. Encapsulation also involves data hiding by restricting access to certain components, which is achieved using access modifiers (e.g., private, protected, and public). This means that the internal representation of an object is hidden from the outside.\n\n   ```python\n   class Car:\n       def __init__(self, make, model):\n           self.__make = make  # private attribute\n           self.model = model  # public attribute\n\n       def get_make(self):\n           return self.__make  # public method to access private attribute\n\n   car = Car(\"Toyota\", \"Corolla\")\n   print(car.get_make())  # Accessing private attribute via public method\n   # print(car.__make)  # This will raise an AttributeError\n   ```\n\n2. **Abstraction**:\n   - Abstraction involves hiding the complex implementation details and showing only the essential features of the object. It enables focusing on what an object does rather than how it does it.\n\n   ```python\n   from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\n   class Animal(ABC):\n       def __init__(self, name):\n           self.name = name\n\n       @abstractmethod\n       def speak(self):\n           pass\n\n   class Dog(Animal):\n       def speak(self):\n           return f\"{self.name} says Woof!\"\n\n   dog = Dog(\"Buddy\")\n   print(dog.speak())  # Focus is on the behavior 'speak' not on how it's implemented\n   ```\n\n3. **Inheritance**:\n   - Inheritance allows a new class to inherit attributes and methods of an existing class. This promotes code reusability and establishes a relationship between the new class (subclass) and the existing class (superclass).\n\n   ```python\n   class Vehicle:\n       def __init__(self, brand, color):\n           self.brand = brand\n           self.color = color\n\n       def describe(self):\n           return f\"This is a {self.color} {self.brand}.\"\n\n   class Motorcycle(Vehicle):\n       def __init__(self, brand, color, type):\n           super().__init",
